1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:01,720
Brought to you in part by Farmers Insurance.

2
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:27,760
The date.

3
00:00:27,761 --> 00:00:31,060
December 6th, 1941.

4
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,160
A peaceful weekend in America.

5
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:46,000
But out in the Pacific, a firestorm was gathering.

6
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:56,880
A task force of Japanese aircraft carriers had slipped to within a few hundred miles

7
00:00:56,881 --> 00:00:59,020
of the U.S. Navy base at Pearl Harbor.

8
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:02,580
At dawn, they launched their warplanes,

9
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:03,720
hundreds of them,

10
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:06,760
in a stunning and deadly surprise attack.

11
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,780
For the United States,

12
00:01:25,781 --> 00:01:30,980
a long period of isolationism had come to a swift and tragic end.

13
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:35,980
World War II, already raging in Europe for two years,

14
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,720
had finally hit home, and a sleeping giant had awakened.

15
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,760
The war would demand great feats of courage

16
00:01:44,761 --> 00:01:47,380
on the battlefields of Europe,

17
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,100
across the vast expanses of the Pacific,

18
00:01:51,101 --> 00:01:54,140
and miles overhead, high in the sky.

19
00:01:55,780 --> 00:02:07,620
It would also require great feats of industrial might,

20
00:02:07,621 --> 00:02:11,140
more manufacturing output than the world had ever seen.

21
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:15,380
Thousands of heavily armed warships to sweep the seas,

22
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:19,860
tens of thousands of tanks to punch through German lines,

23
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,220
and hundreds of thousands of warplanes,

24
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:26,120
Planes enough to darken the skies.

25
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:32,100
In aircraft factories all across America, farmhands and housewives and clerks,

26
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:38,620
more than a million strong, would join forces to build the mightiest air armada ever launched.

27
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:45,160
Lightning-fast fighters to shoot down enemy planes.

28
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:50,080
Deadly bombers to flatten German and Japanese war plants.

29
00:02:50,081 --> 00:02:55,720
and tough, reliable cargo planes to airlift troops and supplies into battle.

30
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:01,480
The challenge to America's young aviation industry was formidable.

31
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:09,260
Between, say, 1919 and 1939, which is generally considered the golden age, the interwar period,

32
00:03:09,261 --> 00:03:16,960
at an average of 2,000 to 3,000 aircraft a year, we're looking at 40,000 to 60,000 aircraft total were produced.

33
00:03:16,961 --> 00:03:22,280
by 1944. In 1944 alone, the United States produced almost 100,000 airplanes.

34
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:28,677
Production was incredible, figures that were highly unimaginable years before.

35
00:03:28,678 --> 00:03:29,400
By the end

36
00:03:29,401 --> 00:03:34,860
of the war, some 300,000 military aircraft had been produced. What this required was

37
00:03:34,861 --> 00:03:40,580
an incredible managerial and organizational ability that no other industry had manifested

38
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:47,775
before then. American warplanes would soon become the stuff of legend.

39
00:03:47,776 --> 00:03:49,420
So would the men

40
00:03:49,421 --> 00:03:50,826
who created them.

41
00:03:50,827 --> 00:03:56,700
Men like Dutch Kindleberger and Lee Atwood, whose Mustang fighter would

42
00:03:56,701 --> 00:03:59,524
decimate the German Luftwaffe.

43
00:03:59,525 --> 00:04:04,700
Roy Grumman, a soft-spoken engineer whose Hellcat would

44
00:04:04,701 --> 00:04:09,427
leap from carrier decks in the Pacific to maul the Japanese Air Force.

45
00:04:09,428 --> 00:04:10,440
Donald Douglas,

46
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:14,360
whose DC-3 airliner would become the military's Gooney Bird,

47
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:17,420
the lifeline of Allied troops throughout the world.

48
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:25,040
And Bill Boeing, whose name marked the bombers that would shatter Hitler's war machine.

49
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:37,680
Ironically, just 20 years before, mere survival was the toughest battle most airplane manufacturers were fighting.

50
00:04:37,681 --> 00:04:46,300
In the early 1920s, barnstormers captivated the nation's fancy with feats of aerial daring.

51
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:54,080
But while airplane pilots enjoyed fame and fortune,

52
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:58,260
airplane manufacturers struggled to find a market for their wares.

53
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:04,140
Surprisingly, the man who finally set the pattern for success was not an aviator,

54
00:05:04,141 --> 00:05:06,660
but a wealthy Northwest timber baron.

55
00:05:06,661 --> 00:05:08,820
His name was Bill Boeing.

56
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:15,180
Bill Boeing was a millionaire many times over, long before he got into the aviation business.

57
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:20,120
He was a financial genius, if you will.

58
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:25,340
He was not a builder in the sense of, he was not a designer or an engineer,

59
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:32,200
but he knew the financial world, and he brought in the best people he could find to run his companies,

60
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,200
which is why his company was so successful.

61
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:40,798
Boeing's big opportunity came in 1925, when the U.S.

62
00:05:40,799 --> 00:05:44,060
Post Office opened the nation's embryonic

63
00:05:44,061 --> 00:05:48,520
airmail routes to private contractors.

64
00:05:48,521 --> 00:05:53,740
Boeing put in a low-ball bid on the longest and hardest route Chicago to San Francisco,

65
00:05:53,741 --> 00:05:59,420
and soon surprised competitors by turning a profit.

66
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:04,322
Soon Boeing started carrying passengers along with the mail, and a pioneering U.S.

67
00:06:04,323 --> 00:06:05,023
airline

68
00:06:05,024 --> 00:06:06,340
was born.

69
00:06:06,341 --> 00:06:13,340
Boeing's air transport subsidiary became United Airlines.

70
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:20,780
In 1933, in the heart of the Great Depression, Bill Boeing unveiled a dazzling new airliner

71
00:06:20,781 --> 00:06:22,780
called the 247.

72
00:06:22,781 --> 00:06:28,980
Well, the Boeing 247 is widely considered to be the first modern airliner. Modern in

73
00:06:28,981 --> 00:06:34,060
the sense that it was all metal construction, cantilevered wings, in other words, had internal

74
00:06:34,061 --> 00:06:40,220
supports retractable landing gear cowled engines controllable pitch propellers major breakthrough

75
00:06:40,221 --> 00:06:46,980
its cruising speed was about 160 miles an hour which was 60 percent faster than contemporary

76
00:06:46,981 --> 00:06:52,860
airliners of the time the likes of the ford trimotor and whatnot the boeing 247 first flew

77
00:06:52,861 --> 00:06:58,720
on february 8th 1933 entered service about a month or so later with united airlines and

78
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:02,200
completely revolutionized their transportation in the United States.

79
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:07,560
Boeing's 247 rendered all other airliners obsolete.

80
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:14,380
Then the Boeing company made a mistake that would haunt it for decades.

81
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:20,300
TWA, one of United's fiercest rivals, ordered 20 of the new airliners.

82
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:23,940
But Boeing refused the order.

83
00:07:24,620 --> 00:07:27,440
The first 60 planes were earmarked for United.

84
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:35,720
Saddled with obsolete planes, TWA desperately sought another builder to produce a competitive model.

85
00:07:35,721 --> 00:07:43,720
Finally, a designer in Los Angeles took the job. His name was Donald Douglas.

86
00:07:43,721 --> 00:07:50,720
Douglas' challenge to the Boeing 247 was a prototype he called the DC-1.

87
00:07:50,721 --> 00:07:52,720
called the DC-1.

88
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:56,220
But he never sold DC-1s.

89
00:07:56,221 --> 00:08:00,720
Instead, he leapfrogged straight to improve production models,

90
00:08:00,721 --> 00:08:06,220
the DC-2, and soon an even better model, the DC-3.

91
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:11,720
The DC-3 introduced wing flaps which added lift,

92
00:08:11,721 --> 00:08:16,220
allowing the plane to use shorter runways or land at slower, safer speeds.

93
00:08:16,221 --> 00:08:21,600
more powerful engines, which gave the plane a top speed of 200 miles an hour,

94
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:28,880
and a 21-passenger cabin, which gave the plane far more earning power than the 10-passenger Boeing design.

95
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:36,220
Virtually every American airline going into World War II was flying DC-3s.

96
00:08:36,221 --> 00:08:39,440
That was the standard American airliner.

97
00:08:39,441 --> 00:08:53,441
Twenty-one passengers would cruise at higher altitudes at greater speed with greater range than any other passenger airliner in the sky.

98
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:04,920
And it had the right economics, the right combination between costs and performance.

99
00:09:04,921 --> 00:09:10,920
So it became the standard by which all other commercial airliners were judged.

100
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:18,340
Even United Airlines, whose monopoly on the Boeing 247 had triggered the Douglas design,

101
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:21,900
replaced its Boeing planes with Douglas airliners.

102
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:29,320
But it was the outbreak of World War II that launched the Douglas plane by the thousands.

103
00:09:29,321 --> 00:09:36,220
We put the small passenger door out on the left side of the airplane

104
00:09:36,221 --> 00:09:38,040
and put in a big door.

105
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:40,140
Then we beefed up the floor structure

106
00:09:40,141 --> 00:09:46,200
so that we could carry cannon or various artillery pieces

107
00:09:46,201 --> 00:09:50,300
and you could actually run a jeep up into the cabin of a DC-3.

108
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:53,140
And the door was wide enough to load cargo.

109
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:56,300
And also part of the door would come off

110
00:09:56,301 --> 00:09:59,120
so they could drop parachute jumpers out of it.

111
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:07,540
Dressed in olive drab, the military version of the DC-3 was officially named the C-47.

112
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:11,120
But soldiers fondly called it the Gooney Bird,

113
00:10:11,121 --> 00:10:15,560
for the ungainliness with which it lumbered into the air with its loaves.

114
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:23,860
The Douglas plane came to dominate military transport, just as it had passenger service.

115
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:27,720
But the Boeing company was not about to sit on the sidelines.

116
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:36,640
Boeing began work on a new plane and came roaring back into the heat of battle with a plane that would become a legend.

117
00:10:40,460 --> 00:10:43,282
Charles Lindbergh's plane, Ryan NYP, the spirit of St.

118
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:47,464
Louis, was named in honor of Lindbergh's supporters and financial backers in St.

119
00:10:47,465 --> 00:10:48,300
Louis, Missouri.

120
00:10:48,980 --> 00:10:52,360
NYP is an acronym for his route, New York to Paris.

