165
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:04,000
We now return to Warplanes of World War II on Modern Marvels.

166
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:11,260
By the late 1930s, Donald Douglas was the nation's foremost aircraft builder.

167
00:14:11,960 --> 00:14:16,540
The success of the DC-3 turned his factory into a beehive of activity.

168
00:14:16,541 --> 00:14:23,460
With the outbreak of war, the beehive became a hornet's nest.

169
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:30,400
But Douglas' rival, Boeing, was also carried aloft on the winds of war.

170
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:36,980
After the untimely death of its 247 commercial airliner,

171
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:41,200
Boeing's engineers concentrated on designing planes for the military.

172
00:14:42,060 --> 00:14:48,700
By 1935, they had unveiled the prototype for a remarkable new bomber, the B-17.

173
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:56,300
The B-17 was big, tough, and armed to the teeth.

174
00:14:58,740 --> 00:15:06,980
Besides 2,000 pounds of bombs, the B-17 boasted gun turrets in its nose, tail, back, and belly.

175
00:15:06,981 --> 00:15:14,820
An awestruck journalist described the prototype as a flying fortress, and the name stuck.

176
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:20,940
Building the bombers was a veritable army of workers.

177
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:24,640
At one point, 45,000 of them.

178
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:27,900
They were recruited wherever they could be found.

179
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:35,060
Soon, the Douglas and Lockheed factories were building B-17s as well.

180
00:15:35,061 --> 00:15:41,680
As more and more men went to war, more and more women came to work.

181
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:53,320
Rosie the Riveter, this group of workers, was called, and by 1943, Rosie's by the thousands were building B-17s.

182
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:59,540
A riveter was one of the gals that would drill a hole and shoot a rivet.

183
00:16:00,180 --> 00:16:03,960
Now, most women are very, very proud of the fact that they worked during the war,

184
00:16:03,961 --> 00:16:05,960
and they like to be called Rosie the Rivet,

185
00:16:06,020 --> 00:16:07,780
but some of them have never even touched a river

186
00:16:07,781 --> 00:16:09,020
and know what a rivet looks like.

187
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:11,300
I am Rosie the Rivet.

188
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:14,280
I drilled that hole and I shot that rivet,

189
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:15,840
and I'm proud of that.

190
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:20,460
The numbers of women in the workforce are very substantial.

191
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:23,980
Our best estimates are that 4.5 million women

192
00:16:23,981 --> 00:16:26,180
that would not have worked for wages before

193
00:16:26,181 --> 00:16:28,740
were in the workforce during World War II.

194
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:30,200
There were very few men.

195
00:16:30,201 --> 00:16:33,200
We had about 98% women in that whole plant.

196
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:34,800
They drove the tugs.

197
00:16:35,320 --> 00:16:39,080
They delivered the aircraft to female civilian pilots out there, you know.

198
00:16:39,440 --> 00:16:41,260
They worked in a machine shop.

199
00:16:41,960 --> 00:16:42,840
I was amazed.

200
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:45,580
But when you're at war and you're fighting for your country,

201
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:49,960
there's nothing you can't do or won't try to do to help win the war.

202
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:51,900
The pace was frantic.

203
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:53,800
The hours were long.

204
00:16:54,460 --> 00:16:56,820
And the work was often exhausting.

205
00:16:57,660 --> 00:17:01,840
But the sense of urgency made fatigue seem unimportant.

206
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:06,680
A B-17 line moved every seven and a half hours.

207
00:17:07,340 --> 00:17:09,840
We built over 3,000 B-17s.

208
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:11,620
That's a lot of ships.

209
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:15,360
I'd come home at night, and man, I was ready to hit the sack.

210
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:18,880
Sure, we were tired, but we knew we had a job to do.

211
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:21,680
And, you know, we weren't sure we were going to win that war.

212
00:17:22,060 --> 00:17:23,560
And if we could contribute to it,

213
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:25,700
it was the best thing in the world could have happened to us.

214
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:31,120
Many Rosies were housewives whose husbands were at war.

215
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:34,860
A few, though, came from a more colorful background.

216
00:17:36,660 --> 00:17:42,520
Near a military base in Alaska, the Army, it seems, shut down several brothels.

217
00:17:44,580 --> 00:17:49,140
To make ends meet, the women moved to Seattle and got jobs at Boeing.

218
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:56,380
But soon they found a way to supplement their paychecks by moonlighting in the air raid shelters beneath the factory.

219
00:17:56,381 --> 00:18:03,520
Morale rose to an all-time high until the women were found out and fired.

220
00:18:06,220 --> 00:18:11,440
Despite such occasional distractions, the makeshift workforce made good.

221
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:18,000
By 1943, Boeing was churning out 16 forts a day, one every 40 minutes.

222
00:18:18,001 --> 00:18:27,080
In Europe, the Army's 8th Air Force put the flying fortress to deadly use

223
00:18:27,081 --> 00:18:38,760
In August of 1943, 376 B-17s left England for a strike on German ball bearing and aircraft factories

224
00:18:38,761 --> 00:18:43,920
It marked the beginning of a campaign of round-the-clock bombing

225
00:18:43,921 --> 00:18:54,240
The Boeing B-17 was at the heart of crippling Allied airstrikes, including massive raids that launched a thousand bombers at a time.

226
00:18:56,580 --> 00:18:59,780
Over Berlin, the Germans came in at us head on.

227
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:09,620
There were some mid-air collisions and there were pieces of airplane and pieces of people that were in the air up there.

228
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:12,980
It was a pretty horrendous thing.

229
00:19:12,981 --> 00:19:19,600
I mean, I'm sure you've seen many pictures and accounts of planes that come back

230
00:19:19,601 --> 00:19:22,700
that looked like they were just absolutely unflyable.

231
00:19:23,440 --> 00:19:27,200
That plane would fly as long as the crew was willing to stay with it.

232
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:38,740
The B-17s inflicted a fearsome toll, not just on the ground, but in the air as well.

233
00:19:38,741 --> 00:19:45,740
B-17 gunner shot down two-thirds of all enemy fighters destroyed during the war.

234
00:19:57,740 --> 00:20:01,740
All told, Boeing built 7,000 B-17s.

235
00:20:01,741 --> 00:20:06,740
Two other companies, Douglas and Lockheed, built 5,000 more.

236
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:12,140
But it wasn't enough.

237
00:20:12,141 --> 00:20:15,300
As the war escalated, it quickly became clear

238
00:20:15,301 --> 00:20:22,020
that mass production on an unimaginable scale was needed.

239
00:20:22,021 --> 00:20:26,000
And so the Pentagon looked to the man whose very name was synonymous

240
00:20:26,001 --> 00:20:30,860
with assembly line production, Henry Ford.

241
00:20:30,861 --> 00:20:35,260
On nearly 2,000 acres of Michigan farmland west of Detroit

242
00:20:35,261 --> 00:20:38,280
by a small stream called Willow Run,

243
00:20:38,281 --> 00:20:44,720
Henry Ford's bulldozers and dump trucks began laying the groundwork for the world's longest assembly line.

244
00:20:45,420 --> 00:20:50,460
It would be a mile-long marvel designed to build bombers' automotive style.

245
00:20:53,160 --> 00:20:58,000
The man who made it happen was Ford's production chief, Charles Sorensen.

246
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:04,280
Charles Sorensen was actually appalled by the methods he saw being used to build aircraft.

247
00:21:04,840 --> 00:21:08,780
It was basically 35 years out of date by automotive standards.

248
00:21:09,640 --> 00:21:15,080
And when asked if he had an idea how to do it, he said, I'll have something for you in the morning.

249
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:23,680
He just took the backside of a calendar pad and sketched out a drawing of what he had in mind for his factory.

250
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:26,533
It was a straight line factory.

251
00:21:26,534 --> 00:21:30,180
Put the materials in one side and fly the airplanes out the other.

252
00:21:30,181 --> 00:21:34,300
They told him it wasn't possible

253
00:21:34,301 --> 00:21:36,720
But you don't know Charlie

254
00:21:36,721 --> 00:21:39,580
It was just like waving a red flag in front of a bull

255
00:21:39,581 --> 00:21:41,060
He had to do it

256
00:21:41,061 --> 00:21:46,780
The factory measured three and a half million square feet

257
00:21:46,781 --> 00:21:48,320
More than 80 acres

258
00:21:48,321 --> 00:21:54,220
Adjoining it, Ford built an immense airport with miles of runway

259
00:21:54,221 --> 00:22:01,220
But the claims intended for those runways were slow in coming.

260
00:22:01,221 --> 00:22:08,220
A year and a half after groundbreaking, the gigantic plant had yielded barely a handful of bombers.

261
00:22:08,221 --> 00:22:12,220
The public was not very patient.

262
00:22:12,221 --> 00:22:17,220
And our troops were dying and we needed the aircraft and they weren't coming yet.

263
00:22:17,221 --> 00:22:22,220
So there was a lot of emotion built up over Willow Run.

264
00:22:22,221 --> 00:22:26,540
A popular headline was the phrase, Will it run?

265
00:22:26,900 --> 00:22:28,280
This was a tremendous investment.

266
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:32,920
It was a multi-million dollar investment on the part of the country in this facility,

267
00:22:33,080 --> 00:22:34,380
and nothing was coming out of it.

268
00:22:34,920 --> 00:22:37,700
And the Ford Motor Company didn't have ready answers.

269
00:22:38,100 --> 00:22:41,540
People thought that once you had a building, you merely flipped a switch

270
00:22:41,541 --> 00:22:44,940
and Finnish bombers flew off into the heavens.

271
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:45,900
It didn't work that way.

272
00:22:45,901 --> 00:22:53,720
But finally, in 1943, the Willow Run plant hit its stride.

273
00:22:54,500 --> 00:22:58,180
The complex assembly line was like a huge freight train.

274
00:22:58,660 --> 00:23:04,000
Although ponderous to set in motion, it gradually acquired astonishing momentum.

275
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:13,460
31 bombers in January, 75 in February, 104 in March, 148 in April.

276
00:23:13,461 --> 00:23:17,300
The mile-long assembly line was gathering steam.

277
00:23:18,800 --> 00:23:24,100
Day and night, an army of workers, including many Rosies, built the bombers.

278
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,800
A few workers had rather unique qualifications.

279
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:33,520
When the police started going down the assembly line, they called center wing, that would be the center,

280
00:23:33,640 --> 00:23:38,680
but the very tip, a normal-sized person could not rivet there, they couldn't get inside.

281
00:23:38,681 --> 00:23:44,740
See, they hired midgets or little people to get inside to buck the rivets.

282
00:23:45,880 --> 00:23:47,860
Because anyone else couldn't do it.

283
00:23:49,140 --> 00:23:51,320
All in all, it's something you couldn't believe.

284
00:23:51,840 --> 00:23:53,620
You look around at all those people there.

285
00:23:54,820 --> 00:23:56,060
Some of them couldn't read or write.

286
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:59,400
Some couldn't even speak English.

287
00:24:00,080 --> 00:24:02,160
But they all put forth an effort you wouldn't believe.

288
00:24:02,260 --> 00:24:04,020
And there was no discord among any of them.

289
00:24:04,021 --> 00:24:14,760
By the end of 1943, with 46,000 workers on the assembly line and another 22,000 making parts off-site,

290
00:24:15,480 --> 00:24:18,920
Willow Run was spitting out 12 new bombers a day.

291
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:23,700
The new plane was called the B-24 Liberator.

292
00:24:24,640 --> 00:24:29,060
It was faster than the B-17, and it could fly farther.

293
00:24:29,061 --> 00:24:35,540
it had the range on it, the B-24 was about 3,000 miles, whereas the B-17 is around 2,000 miles.

294
00:24:36,340 --> 00:24:39,560
But the bomb loads were practically the same.

295
00:24:39,860 --> 00:24:44,480
But I found it had a few drawbacks because the fuel line seemed to run through the bomb bay,

296
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:50,240
and if those little connections weren't made exactly so, that airplane could go kabooms,

297
00:24:50,300 --> 00:24:51,680
which some of them did, you know.

298
00:24:52,640 --> 00:24:55,880
It was not a popular airplane.

299
00:24:56,060 --> 00:24:57,780
It was not easy to fly.

300
00:24:57,781 --> 00:24:59,680
It was a hard airplane.

301
00:24:59,880 --> 00:25:01,060
You had to wrestle it.

302
00:25:01,140 --> 00:25:09,980
At the end of an eight-hour mission, you got out and your legs feel like somebody had just jabbed big burning needles up into them because of that constant work.

303
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:11,720
But it kept bringing us back.

304
00:25:12,860 --> 00:25:21,360
And gradually, whatever shortcomings it may have possessed, the Liberator more than made up for in sheer numbers and range.

305
00:25:21,361 --> 00:25:28,600
All told, American factories built more than 18,000 B-24s

306
00:25:28,601 --> 00:25:31,700
More than any other airplane produced during the war

307
00:25:31,701 --> 00:25:35,480
Ford alone assembled 6,800

308
00:25:35,481 --> 00:25:40,500
And shipped another 1,900 in pieces to other factories for assembly

309
00:25:40,501 --> 00:25:47,380
Henry Ford and his assembly line had joined up and earned their stripes

310
00:25:47,381 --> 00:25:59,300
By the latter half of 1943, wave upon wave of American-built bombers were striking deep into the heart of Nazi territory.

311
00:26:02,060 --> 00:26:08,500
But the deeper the bombers penetrated, the more vulnerable they became to Luftwaffe fighters.

312
00:26:10,640 --> 00:26:13,840
The liberators and fortresses needed protection.

313
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:19,800
Soon, there emerged a revolutionary new fighter plane provided.

314
