511
00:40:36,240 --> 00:40:40,920
We now return to Warplanes of World War II on Modern Marvels.

512
00:40:40,921 --> 00:40:44,968
In the bloody battle for the Pacific, the U.S.

513
00:40:44,969 --> 00:40:52,360
finally evened the score with Japan at the Battle of Midway, fought in June of 1942.

514
00:40:58,340 --> 00:41:06,140
There, Navy pilots sank three Japanese carriers and tripled a fourth, and Japan lost nearly 300 planes.

515
00:41:06,141 --> 00:41:11,980
It was a sign of things to come

516
00:41:11,981 --> 00:41:18,460
The war in the Pacific marked the combat debut of the aircraft carrier

517
00:41:18,461 --> 00:41:21,080
For both Japan and the United States

518
00:41:21,081 --> 00:41:23,780
And it was a most convincing debut

519
00:41:23,781 --> 00:41:29,680
Carrier-based airplanes swept the skies for hundreds of miles around their ships

520
00:41:29,681 --> 00:41:35,020
And the major naval battles of the Pacific were won or lost by air power

521
00:41:35,021 --> 00:41:46,940
A pivotal role in those battles was played by planes from a Long Island factory known as the Iron Works, Grumman Aircraft.

522
00:41:48,840 --> 00:41:53,640
It was the creation of a soft-spoken engineer named Roy Grumman.

523
00:41:53,641 --> 00:42:07,641
My father was the conceptual engineering type who had the ideas and had a genius for the mechanics of flight and what it took to make an airplane perform the way it

524
00:42:08,333 --> 00:42:09,033
should.

525
00:42:10,340 --> 00:42:17,740
A former Navy test pilot, Grumman had a superb grasp of the unique demands imposed by aircraft carriers.

526
00:42:17,741 --> 00:42:25,740
His first Navy fighter was a two-seater, but it could outfly the Navy's fastest single-seat fighter.

527
00:42:28,640 --> 00:42:31,080
The Navy ordered 27 of them.

528
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:37,940
Grumman expanded from a tiny garage into a real factory on Long Island.

529
00:42:40,180 --> 00:42:44,720
By 1940, Grumman was on his fourth major fighter design for the Navy.

530
00:42:44,721 --> 00:42:50,920
Officially designated the F-4F fighter, it was called the Wildcat

531
00:42:50,921 --> 00:42:58,260
It owed its most innovative feature to a rubber eraser and a pair of paper clips

532
00:42:58,261 --> 00:43:05,240
A space-saving folding wing, which doubled the number of planes a carrier could hold

533
00:43:06,240 --> 00:43:11,340
So my father had been thinking about this, he said for some time without success

534
00:43:11,341 --> 00:43:16,040
To help him visualize this, he actually took two paper clips and an eraser,

535
00:43:16,600 --> 00:43:20,300
and by sticking this into an eraser at different angles,

536
00:43:20,820 --> 00:43:26,760
he was able to get the wing to fold neatly along the side of the plane.

537
00:43:27,200 --> 00:43:30,960
And he made this model where the wings folded, and he put it in a box,

538
00:43:31,080 --> 00:43:34,020
and he trundled off to Washington carrying this box,

539
00:43:34,120 --> 00:43:38,240
and he sat down with the Bureau of Aeronautics people, and he opened the box.

540
00:43:38,241 --> 00:43:45,180
He took out the plane with the wings folded, put it on the table, and then spread the wings out to their position.

541
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:51,080
And in his words, the Bureau of Aeronautics people were delighted and amazed.

542
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:58,026
After Pearl Harbor, with the Navy fighting a two-ocean war, U.S.

543
00:43:58,027 --> 00:44:01,820
shipyards were building new aircraft carriers by the dozens.

544
00:44:04,000 --> 00:44:07,560
Demand for Wildcats proved almost insatiable.

545
00:44:08,240 --> 00:44:17,240
Grumman's manufacturing complex grew from one plant to two, then three, sprawling over 120 acres.

546
00:44:18,440 --> 00:44:20,900
Production lines ran 20 hours a day.

547
00:44:22,400 --> 00:44:27,940
During the long, bloody battle for Guadalcanal, even top speed wasn't fast enough.

548
00:44:29,080 --> 00:44:35,600
Grumman Aircraft Company was going full bore on a production line producing these Wildcats.

549
00:44:35,601 --> 00:44:40,220
and it so happened that they had a bunch of parts for Wildcats

550
00:44:40,221 --> 00:44:43,200
all crated up and ready to send out to the carriers

551
00:44:43,201 --> 00:44:45,520
for use as replacement parts.

552
00:44:46,460 --> 00:44:48,600
And when the Navy put in a call to the company

553
00:44:48,601 --> 00:44:52,300
desperately asking for more of these planes as quickly as possible,

554
00:44:52,800 --> 00:44:55,240
the company actually came in on a weekend

555
00:44:55,241 --> 00:45:00,520
and opened the crates and put together 24 Wildcats

556
00:45:00,521 --> 00:45:02,640
which were immediately shipped out to the fleet.

557
00:45:02,641 --> 00:45:07,320
The Wildcat was a strappy fighter.

558
00:45:08,280 --> 00:45:15,460
One day, a lone Wildcat pilot named Butch O'Hare gave a powerful demonstration of what the airplane could do.

559
00:45:16,840 --> 00:45:20,177
Butch O'Hare was a young Lieutenant J.G.

560
00:45:20,178 --> 00:45:27,520
who had been in the combat air patrol over the Lexington during the Battle of Coral Sea.

561
00:45:27,521 --> 00:45:33,480
and he was the only airplane airborne when a group of Japanese airplanes came in,

562
00:45:33,680 --> 00:45:37,160
and bombers, and he single-handedly shot down six of them

563
00:45:37,161 --> 00:45:39,500
right over the carrier inside of everybody.

564
00:45:39,740 --> 00:45:40,840
They all saw it happen.

565
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:42,520
For that, he received the Medal of Honor.

566
00:45:45,840 --> 00:45:50,600
But against Japanese fighter planes, the Wildcat had a tougher time.

567
00:45:51,840 --> 00:45:56,880
The Japanese Zero had surprised the Americans with its speed and maneuverability.

568
00:45:57,520 --> 00:46:03,100
The Zero wasn't as tough as the Wildcat, but in dogfight after dogfight,

569
00:46:03,480 --> 00:46:06,580
the Japanese fighter proved faster and more nimble.

570
00:46:08,360 --> 00:46:12,320
The standard tactic in those days, they had some very experienced Japanese pilots,

571
00:46:12,321 --> 00:46:17,260
and their standing evasion tactic, if you jumped one of them, was they immediately went into a loop.

572
00:46:17,980 --> 00:46:22,820
And if you followed them through the loop in a Wildcat, on the second loop, they would be behind you.

573
00:46:22,821 --> 00:46:27,900
The ironworks got busy on a new and better fighter.

574
00:46:29,280 --> 00:46:34,620
On August 31st, 1943, in the skies above Marcus Island,

575
00:46:35,120 --> 00:46:40,120
that new fighter made its combat debut, the Grumman Hellcat.

576
00:46:43,660 --> 00:46:49,280
Roy Grumman soon learned that the Japanese Zero finally met its match.

577
00:46:49,281 --> 00:47:01,400
Yes, I remember receiving a wire after the Hellcat squadrons were fighting from one of the squadron commanders saying that their ratio was 200 to 1.

578
00:47:01,920 --> 00:47:05,580
In other words, 200 Jap planes shot down to the loss of one Hellcat.

579
00:47:07,500 --> 00:47:11,540
In battle after battle, the Hellcat burned its name.

580
00:47:13,760 --> 00:47:18,900
The Navy clamored for more, and Grumman responded with more production lines.

581
00:47:19,280 --> 00:47:26,080
Grumman's workforce soon topped 25,000

582
00:47:26,081 --> 00:47:28,560
and a third of the workers were women.

583
00:47:30,440 --> 00:47:32,500
Some built Hellcats,

584
00:47:32,920 --> 00:47:35,540
others test-flew the powerful planes.

585
00:47:37,580 --> 00:47:39,620
To keep women on the job,

586
00:47:39,900 --> 00:47:41,460
Grumman opened daycare centers.

587
00:47:42,460 --> 00:47:45,040
To keep wounded soldiers alive,

588
00:47:45,041 --> 00:47:47,400
the company held regular blood drives.

589
00:47:47,401 --> 00:47:52,340
To keep morale high, there were musical concerts at lunchtime.

590
00:47:53,380 --> 00:47:59,160
To keep employees fit, the company organized daily calisthenics and intramural softball.

591
00:48:01,940 --> 00:48:08,300
The strategy worked. Absenteeism and resignations dropped. Productivity soared.

592
00:48:10,080 --> 00:48:16,580
The 12,000 Hellcats built by Grumman mauled the Japanese Air Force mercilessly.

593
00:48:17,400 --> 00:48:24,400
US carrier planes destroyed nearly 6,500 Japanese aircraft.

594
00:48:24,401 --> 00:48:30,400
80% of those were downed by Hellcats.

595
00:48:30,401 --> 00:48:39,400
Even if they didn't win every dogfight, Hellcats almost always survived them.

596
00:48:39,401 --> 00:48:45,400
Sometimes they made it home against staggering odds.

597
00:48:45,401 --> 00:48:46,101
nods.

598
00:48:46,102 --> 00:48:52,900
There was a tremendous explosion and I looked out to the left and almost fainted because

599
00:48:52,901 --> 00:48:54,909
about 80 percent of the wing was gone.

600
00:48:54,910 --> 00:48:58,080
I noticed in the mirror that my port elevator and stabilizer

601
00:48:58,081 --> 00:49:01,505
were completely gone off the airplane on the left side.

602
00:49:01,506 --> 00:49:04,120
Make a long story short, I was able to fly

603
00:49:04,121 --> 00:49:06,423
that airplane back out over the fleet.

604
00:49:06,424 --> 00:49:10,060
About that time they had an airplane come up and join me and

605
00:49:10,061 --> 00:49:13,456
he looked at me and he said, did you know your left wheel is gone?

606
00:49:13,457 --> 00:49:15,000
He said, no, he said, but your

607
00:49:15,001 --> 00:49:15,987
hook is down.

608
00:49:15,988 --> 00:49:21,380
I made a nice gentle carrier landing and caught a wire and even with no

609
00:49:21,381 --> 00:49:26,320
wheel on the left it just stopped on the deck. We flew that airplane ten days later.

610
00:49:27,960 --> 00:49:32,289
Finally, in August of 1945, it ended.

611
00:49:32,290 --> 00:49:38,140
Another new American plane, a solitary Boeing B-29

612
00:49:38,141 --> 00:49:45,520
super fortress took off from a Pacific air base, flew high over Hiroshima, Japan, and

613
00:49:45,521 --> 00:49:50,280
dropped a bomb that would end the war and change the world forever.

614
